 ## Overview
-Valley Cell Simplification is a topological noise reduction method that removes insignificant "valley cells" from a decomposition of a scalar field (typically a 2D density plot for transfer function design) by merging adjacent cells based on boundary persistence. It solves the problem of over-segmentation caused by noise, where small, semantically irrelevant features fragment the data space. Unlike standard watershed transformations which produce a flat partition, this method operates hierarchically, allowing the user to control the level of detail via a persistence threshold, effectively filtering out noise while preserving significant peaks [S1].
